myanmar's senior general  minong lai arriving in jakarta ahead of  saturday's summit  his invitation has caused uproar in  myanmar granting legitimacy to the coup  say protesters that anger was reflected  by demonstrators outside the asean  secretariat compound  the ferocity of the military crackdown  and the rising death toll  has drawn condemnation around the world  but as the southeast asian leaders  gathered it was clearly going to be a  testing day  splits have emerged in the group  malaysia indonesia and singapore  have been openly critical others most  significantly thailand  have been relatively quiet since the  coup  but as they went into closed session it  was clearly not going to be an easy ride  for myanmar's senior general  in that meeting i said several things  one the development of the situation in  myanmar is unacceptable  and should not continue violence must be  stopped  democracy stability and peace in myanmar  must be returned immediately  what has emerged from the summit is a  five-point plan  calling for an end to the violence and  the release of  all political prisoners  he must restrain himself and his forces  so that tension could be relieved  a request for a second commitment an  inclusive dialogue process must begin  immediately  political prisoners must be released and  an asean special envoy  needs to be formed namely the secretary  general of asean and the chair of asean  to encourage dialogue  with all parties in myanmar but will the  military comply  asean has tried to reign in myanmar's  military before  in the 90s and early 2000s to little  avail on the streets of darwin  protesters were back out once again as  they were in many of myanmar's cities  first in convoys of motorbikes and then  on foot  they will also be hard to placate having  shed so much blood  and will they accept returning to a  system where their democracy  is overseen by the military tony chang  al jazeera
 

